<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bedouin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bedouins, Bedu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bedouin are the nomadic and semi-nomadic Arab tribes of the deserts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, organised in lineages and tribal confederations. Herding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, sheep and goats, they controlled desert routes and served settled states as soldiers, allies or rivals. Several medieval dynasties, such as the Numayrids, Mirdasids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylids</a>, rose from Bedouin tribes, and tribal power remained central to Arabian politics into the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bedouin.jpg" alt="Bedouin" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_bedouins_sheikhs,_c_1867-1876_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
